{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>A dialog interrupts the flow to demand a decision or a short piece of
     input. It is the most expensive surface in an interface — everything on
     screen goes inert behind it — so it must be the only thing the user can
     act on while open.</p>
  <h3>Do use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Destructive confirmations</strong> — "Delete conversation?"
        with Cancel and a danger action.</li>
    <li><strong>Short forms</strong> — rename, invite, move-to; a single
        <a href="text-fields.html">field</a> plus actions. Enter submits via
        <code>method="dialog"</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Decisions that block the flow</strong> — unsaved-changes
        warnings before navigation.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Non-blocking feedback</strong> — "Message sent" is a
        snackbar, not a dialog; never make the user click an OK to dismiss
        good news.</li>
    <li><strong>Long or exploratory content</strong> — release notes,
        settings, browsing. That is the <a href="bottom-sheet.html">bottom
        sheet</a> or a normal page; dialogs cap at ~88vh for a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Chaining decisions</strong> — a wizard behind three OKs is a
        page, badly compressed.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "The hard parts are platform work — by design",
    text: "showModal() provides the focus trap, the inert background, Escape-to-close and top-layer stacking natively. Flint deliberately ships no custom focus management: the classic hand-rolled overlay is 300 lines of fragile JS. flint.js only wires data-fl-open / data-fl-close and returns focus to the invoker on every close path. Open and close animations are pure CSS (@starting-style + allow-discrete).",
    href: "text-fields.html",
    linkText: "Field family"
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-types">Types</h2>
  <p>Material 2 defines four dialog types. Flint ships them as one surface
     plus three modifiers — the differences are content and layout, not
     separate components.</p>
  <table class="docs-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Alert</strong></td>
        <td>Title + body + actions (the default, no modifier)</td>
        <td>One decision that blocks the flow — "Delete conversation?",
            unsaved-changes warnings</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Simple</strong></td>
        <td>Title + vertical option list
            (<code>fl-dialog__list</code> + <code>fl-dialog__option</code>);
            picking a row closes with that row's value</td>
        <td>Choosing from a small set without a form — "Apply label", pick an
            account</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Confirmation</strong></td>
        <td>Question title + dismissive/confirming pair; when the decision
            needs a choice, radio/checkbox rows in the list and the confirm
            stays <code>disabled</code> until one is made</td>
        <td>"Filter by priority?" — decide, then confirm</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Full-screen</strong></td>
        <td><code>fl-dialog--fullscreen</code> + <code>fl-dialog__header</code>
            (close, title, confirming action in the top bar); body scrolls</td>
        <td>The mobile form pattern — compose, settings sub-pages; content too
            big for a card</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Rule of thumb: <em>one</em> decision → alert; pick <em>one of N</em> →
     simple; choose <em>then</em> confirm → confirmation; the content is a
     real form → full-screen (or a <a href="bottom-sheet.html">bottom
     sheet</a>, when it isn't destructive).</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <div class="docs-anatomy">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 260" role="img" aria-label="Dialog anatomy diagram">
      <rect x="1" y="1" width="598" height="258" fill="#5f6368" opacity="0.12"/>
      <!-- surface -->
      <rect x="90" y="40" width="420" height="170" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#dadce0"/>
      <!-- title -->
      <rect x="110" y="62" width="150" height="12" rx="6" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <!-- body text -->
      <rect x="110" y="88" width="360" height="8" rx="4" fill="#9aa0a6"/>
      <rect x="110" y="104" width="300" height="8" rx="4" fill="#9aa0a6"/>
      <!-- actions -->
      <rect x="366" y="180" width="60" height="20" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="#dadce0"/>
      <rect x="376" y="187" width="40" height="6" rx="3" fill="#1a73e8"/>
      <rect x="440" y="180" width="50" height="20" rx="4" fill="#dadce0"/>
      <rect x="450" y="187" width="30" height="6" rx="3" fill="#ffffff"/>
      <!-- callouts -->
      <g font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="700">
        <g><circle cx="78" cy="125" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="78" y="129" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">1</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="300" cy="45" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="300" y="49" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">2</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="300" cy="97" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="300" y="101" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">3</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="300" cy="190" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="300" y="194" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">4</text></g>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Surface</strong> — the card vocabulary: <code>--fl-radius-card</code>,
          1px border, 24px padding. Flex column; long bodies scroll inside
          while title and actions stay pinned.</li>
      <li><strong>Title</strong> — the headline size (24px/400), wired with
          <code>aria-labelledby</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Body</strong> — supporting text, or a
          <a href="text-fields.html">field</a> for form dialogs.</li>
      <li><strong>Actions</strong> — right-aligned; Cancel as a text button,
          the confirm as filled — or <code>fl-btn--danger</code>
          (<code>--fl-error</code>) for destructive confirms.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-actions">Actions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Order:</strong> the dismissive action sits left of the
        confirming action — keep that DOM order; it is also the tab order and
        the visual order.</li>
    <li><strong>Acknowledgment carries one action.</strong> An alert that only
        informs gets a single OK / Got it — no decorative Cancel beside it.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm only when a choice exists.</strong> In confirmation
        dialogs with radio/checkbox rows, keep the confirming action
        <code>disabled</code> until a choice is made — one
        <code>change</code> listener in author code (snippet below).</li>
    <li><strong>Stacked actions:</strong> long labels or narrow surfaces get
        <code>fl-dialog__actions--stacked</code> — full-width buttons with the
        confirming action <em>above</em> the dismissive one. DOM order is
        unchanged (dismiss first, confirm second); the modifier reverses the
        visual order.</li>
    <li><strong>Buttons:</strong> Cancel is a text button, the confirm is
        filled, and destructive confirms are
        <code>fl-btn--danger</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-dismissal">Dismissal</h2>
  <p>By default a dialog closes on Escape and on a scrim click — flint.js
     wires both, and focus returns to the invoker on every close path. Some
     dialogs must be answered: an unsaved-changes warning whose only options
     are "Keep" and "Discard" makes no sense to dismiss by tapping the void.</p>
  <p>Add <code>data-fl-dismissible="false"</code> to the <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>
     and both dismissal paths are refused (Escape via the native
     <code>cancel</code> event, scrim clicks in the wiring), while explicit
     actions keep working — <code>data-fl-close</code> buttons and
     <code>method="dialog"</code> submits are unaffected.</p>
  <p><strong>Use it sparingly.</strong> A dialog the user cannot leave must
     still offer a safe way out — a Cancel/Keep action — or it is a trap.
     Non-dismissible is for decisions, never for information.</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Markup contract:</strong> any element with
        <code>data-fl-open="dialog-id"</code> opens it;
        <code>data-fl-close</code> inside the dialog closes it; or close it
        yourself with <code>flint.dialog.close(el)</code> /
        <code>el.close()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Cancel/confirm results are free:</strong> put the actions in
        <code>&lt;form method="dialog"&gt;</code> — submitting closes the
        dialog and records the pressed button's <code>value</code> in
        <code>dialog.returnValue</code>. Read it after close; no extra JS.
        Buttons outside the form can still submit it with the
        <code>form</code> attribute.</li>
    <li><strong>Focus:</strong> moves to the first focusable control on open;
        returns to the invoker on every close path (button, Escape, scrim
        click, submit).</li>
    <li><strong>Dismissal:</strong> Escape and scrim clicks both close —
        unless the dialog opts out with
        <code>data-fl-dismissible="false"</code> (see
        <a href="#d-dismissal">Dismissal</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Stacking:</strong> a dialog can open over a
        <a href="bottom-sheet.html">bottom sheet</a> (or another dialog) —
        the top layer stacks them in DOM order and Escape peels only the top
        one.</li>
    <li><strong>Scroll lock:</strong> the page locks while any overlay is
        open, restored on close.</li>
    <li><strong>Animation:</strong> 150ms fade + scale in, slide/scale out,
        scrim fading on the same clock — all CSS,
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> disables it entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>No-JS fallback (honest):</strong> without flint.js the dialog
        stays hidden and the page keeps working — the content is not lost,
        it just can't be opened. Call <code>showModal()</code> yourself with
        one line, or drive it from your own code; the CSS has no JS
        dependencies. See the <a href="overlay.html">overlay infra
        demo</a> for the raw lifecycle.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Give the dialog <code>aria-labelledby</code> pointing at its title —
        the screen reader announces the dialog by name.</li>
    <li>The inert background is native: nothing behind the dialog is
        focusable, clickable or announced. No <code>aria-hidden</code> is
        juggled on the page — the classic bug doesn't exist here.</li>
    <li>Focus starts on the first control (usually Cancel — the safe choice)
        and cycles within the dialog. Keep Cancel first in the DOM.</li>
    <li><code>fl-btn--danger</code> uses <code>--fl-error</code> (#d93025,
        4.9:1 on white — AA for its 14px/500 text).</li>
    <li>Don't remove the focus ring; the global <code>:focus-visible</code>
        ring is what makes the trap visible to keyboard users.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

